1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,220
You step onto a Jakarta commuter
train that started its working life in

2
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:04,700
Tokyo decades ago.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,039
In November, Jakarta
refused to buy any more.

4
00:00:09,113 --> 00:00:11,296
A new car cost ten to twelve billion.

5
00:00:11,296 --> 00:00:14,393
Same seats, same doors,
about one tenth of the price.

6
00:00:15,533 --> 00:00:18,174
The first size that matches is the rails.

7
00:00:18,174 --> 00:00:23,017
Japan's regular lines and Java's lines
are both one thousand and sixty-seven

8
00:00:23,017 --> 00:00:24,212
millimetres apart.

9
00:00:24,212 --> 00:00:27,168
Change the rail width
and you need new wheels,

10
00:00:27,168 --> 00:00:29,053
new axles, often a new train.

11
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:31,744
The power matches too.

12
00:00:31,744 --> 00:00:36,308
Jakarta's electric line opened in
1925 on fifteen hundred volts DC,

13
00:00:36,308 --> 00:00:39,998
the same system the 205
series was built for in Tokyo.

14
00:00:41,133 --> 00:00:42,873
Same rails, same power.

15
00:00:43,813 --> 00:00:47,053
A Tokyo train arrives by ship,
gets new signs and checks,

16
00:00:47,473 --> 00:00:49,913
and goes into service with
no rebuilding of the tracks.
